concept of a normal (i.e., perpendicular) vector to characterize the direction
of a plane is convenient, so the Euclidean parts of x and n (i.e., x and n) do
feel metric. It is the extra dimension  for the point that is really awkward
to consider as part of a Euclidean metric. For instance, one should not take
the inner product of two point representatives, which would involve a term
 ·  that is ungeometrical. Perhaps it should be zero – but that would make
the metric no longer Euclidean.

